        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most home buyers, buy motorized.</strong> A cheap flat
            manual treadmill forces a forward-leaning, push-the-belt gait that makes walking
            awkward and running close to impractical, and most end up unused. If the budget is
            tight, a used motorized machine is usually the better buy.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A <strong class="text-white">curved non-motorized trainer</strong> is a different
            proposition — excellent for sprints and intervals, and priced as commercial equipment.
            Buy one for that job, not as a cheaper treadmill. Our
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">spending guide</a>
